{"skill":{"slug":"openclaw-agent-browser","displayName":"Agent Browser"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1772156126394,"changelog":"Initial release — headless browser automation CLI for AI agents","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":4174,"sha256":"7ce2b361056a1d8b1dcebc0aebfae510c2c7047dc2ebd716e05670efbb16671b","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/commands.md","size":4871,"sha256":"89484b8ccdd0d42d466793550de27a1d4bec88dc2af322e57ba4c8fc2ee387b1","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/setup.sh","size":1079,"sha256":"c144207f15a59f4c7d0a578ef19f401a6b77f1e2961c523c5a1dc075f8df27c9","contentType":"application/x-sh"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2636,"sha256":"40594eca71fd674ca6af8880516bffc9f1f5aded2f8cabe2d17faf0ba5546463","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789088460585,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"58f97cc4ab21e9e2e4a90241388a817365734e5e9311cfe7a6bf279d904a9476","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/58f97cc4ab21e9e2e4a90241388a817365734e5e9311cfe7a6bf279d904a9476","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: openclaw-agent-browser\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe OpenClaw AgentSkills bundle is classified as suspicious due to the extensive and high-risk capabilities exposed by the `agent-browser` tool, as detailed in `SKILL.md` and `references/commands.md`. Specifically, the `agent-browser eval \"...\"` command allows the AI agent to execute arbitrary JavaScript within the browser context, enabling potential data exfiltration (e.g., cookies, local storage, sensitive DOM content) and unauthorized network requests. Furthermore, commands like `agent-browser upload`, `agent-browser download`, and `agent-browser --allow-file-access open file:///...` grant the agent broad access to the local file system, posing risks for data exfiltration or unauthorized file manipulation. While these are designed features for browser automation, they create severe vulnerabilities for prompt injection attacks against the AI agent, allowing it to perform actions far beyond its intended scope if not strictly controlled by the OpenClaw runtime.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779167546990},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":63,"severity":"HIGH","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":11,"checkedAt":1789088460585},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill is a legitimate browser automation helper, but it needs Review because it handles credentials, reusable browser sessions, existing Chrome profiles, local files, arbitrary page JavaScript, and unpinned global installation without enough safeguards.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The command reference documents uploads, downloads, recording, arbitrary JavaScript eval, existing-Chrome attachment, and local file access; these are disclosed but under-scoped for an agent-facing skill.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The setup script installs agent-browser globally from npm without a pinned version, then runs agent-browser install to retrieve Chromium and possibly OS dependencies, with no confirmation or integrity controls.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The trigger is very broad and includes login, downloads, form submission, monitoring, and any programmatic web interaction without requiring explicit confirmation for sensitive actions.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill documents auth vault credential reuse and browser state save/load for cookies and localStorage, including auto-save/restore, without strong warnings or consent boundaries.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose coherently covers navigation, form filling, scraping, screenshots, downloads, and web testing; the more powerful commands are browser-automation related but materially expand user impact.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable giving an agent broad browser-control capability. Avoid saving credentials or auth state unless necessary, protect any saved state files like passwords, do not attach it to your everyday Chrome profile, and review the global installer or use a pinned, isolated installation.","findings":"[E4] expected: The referenced recording command writes a browser-session recording; I did not find artifact evidence that it sends agent conversation context to an external service, so this supports a privacy warning rather than a data-exfiltration verdict.\n[SQP-1] unexpected: The broad trigger language is artifact-backed and materially increases the chance of unnecessary browser automation for sensitive web actions.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: The examples for credentials, auth state, recording, Chrome connection, and local file access are real and lack adequate warnings about secrets, session tokens, screen contents, and filesystem exposure.\n[SDI-2] unexpected: Auth vault, arbitrary JavaScript eval, and existing Chrome/CDP connection are documented capabilities that can cross trust boundaries if used by an agent without explicit user control.\n[T08] unexpected: The setup script's global unpinned npm install and dynamic browser/dependency installation are confirmed in the artifact and create supply-chain and host-modification risk.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789088460585}}}}}